-- title:
--   Sharp Riemann–Roch on the full modular function field
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $N$ a nonzero natural number, and let $F = F_{K,N}$ denote [`ModularCurve.modularFunctionFieldFullC K N`](def/ModularCurve_X0ModL.html#L100), the intermediate field of the Laurent series field $K((q))$ over $K$ generated by the set of series $\mathrm{qExpand}\,K\,d\,(\mathrm{jqModC}\,K)$ as $d$ runs over the nonzero divisors of $N$ — that is, the subfield of $K((q))$ obtained by adjoining to $K$ the $q$-expansions of the $j$-function reparametrised by $q \mapsto q^{d}$ for $d \mid N$. Let $D$ be a divisor of $F$ over $K$ in the sense of this development: a finitely supported function from the places of $F$ over $K$ (valuation subrings of $F$ containing the image of $K$, proper in $F$, with principal ideals) to $\mathbb{Z}$, its degree being $\sum_v D(v)\,\deg v$. Write $g$ for [`AlgebraicCurve.genusFF K F`](def/AlgebraicCurve_Repartitions.html#L145), the $K$-dimension of the first cohomology $H^1$ of the zero divisor, and $\ell(D)$ for the $K$-dimension of the Riemann–Roch space of $D$. The hypothesis is $2g - 1 \le \deg D$ as integers, and the conclusion is the equality of integers $\ell(D) = \deg D + 1 - g$.
--
--   This is the sharp form of the Riemann–Roch theorem, valid once the degree exceeds $2g-2$, specialised to the function field generated by the $q$-expansions of $j(q^{d})$, $d \mid N$, over an algebraically closed base field. It serves as the Riemann–Roch input for the constructions of sections of divisors on this field, in particular for the existence of functions in Riemann–Roch spaces with prescribed residues and for hyperplane-section estimates used in the reduction theory of modular curves.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_ell_eq_degree_add_one_sub_genusFF_modularFunctionFieldFullC.lean

